Found under k24. The POD of binary_modules says "A trailing ::* takes the whole namespace", but pp (PAR::Packer 1.064 pp.pm) takes only one level for -M Foo::* (not Foo itself, not Foo::Bar::Quux); the whole namespace is -M Foo:: or -M Foo::**. require-gate.pl on the other hand treats Glob::* at any depth (lib/Foo/.+\.pm). Decide: correct the POD, or translate the glob form for pp (e.g. Foo::* -> Foo::**), and keep require-gate.pl consistent with it.

2026-11-10 03:29 Decision: binary_modules Foo::* goes to pp as -M Foo::** (every module below Foo at any depth, not Foo itself). That is the namespace require-gate.pl already checks for Glob::* (any lib/Glob/.+\.pm, Glob.pm alone does not count), so require-gate.pl stays unchanged. Why not -M Foo:: (Foo plus Foo::**): pp dies when Foo.pm is missing. Checked with pp 1.064 in scratch: 'Cannot find module Foo.pm (specified with -M)', exit 2, no binary. Pure namespaces (plugin dirs without a base module) would then be unusable, and the two glob vocabularies would differ. Anyone who wants Foo too lists Foo next to Foo::*. Mapping s/::\*\z/::**/: Foo::**, Foo:: and plain modules go to pp unchanged. Both -M sites go through a twin helper with an identical body: pp_module_args in .github/dzil-binary/pp-args.pl and _pp_module_args in Binary.pm (same pattern as pp_args_lines/_pp_args_lines). pp 1.064 source: PAR/Packer.pm pack_manifest_hash, -M loop ~l.678-697 (::* -> Module::ScanDeps::_glob_in_inc_1, one level; ::** -> _glob_in_inc, all depths; :: -> _name2moddata(Foo) + ::**), then l.749 _die 'Cannot find module ... (specified with -M)'. An empty namespace packs nothing at exit 0 (checked); binary_require catches that. 1.031: Changes 1.031 (2016-04-10) 'pp now accepts -M Foo::, -M Foo:*, -M Foo:**'. The -M loop in the 1.031 Packer.pm (metacpan source) matches 1.064 line for line, so recommends PAR::Packer 1.031 stays.

